  abstract: >-
    Application-specific integrated circuit design remains inaccessible to
    non-experts because it requires specialized design environments and
    extensive domain expertise. Although gate arrays enable rapid and low-cost
    ASIC prototyping through prefabrication and abstraction, existing design
    tools still rely on expert-oriented workflows and limit their
    accessibility. This paper presents a web-based ASIC design tool for
    gate-array-based prototyping. The proposed system allows users to design
    circuits, perform SPICE simulation, and visualize chip layouts entirely
    within a web browser while hiding process-dependent confidential
    manufacturing information from end users. As a feasibility demonstration,
    we designed and fabricated a gate-array-based ASIC implementing a 31-stage
    ring oscillator composed of 132 MOS transistors. The complete process from
    design data generation to chip fabrication was completed within six hours.
    These results demonstrate the potential of browser-based environments to
    democratize rapid ASIC prototyping.
